diff --git a/docs/DAFEIYU_REPLAY.md b/docs/DAFEIYU_REPLAY.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c35f8dc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/DAFEIYU_REPLAY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+# Local replay pilot
+
+The pilot configuration `vite.dafeiyu.config.ts` binds only to loopback HTTP on port 15184. Start it with `pnpm exec vite --config vite.dafeiyu.config.ts` after preparing the normal dependencies. Original user-supplied game resources remain required.
+
+`ui.nextBattle` accepts `commandId`, `expectedPageId`, `expectedSessionId`, and `replaceSession: true`. It requires the specified session's native victory or defeat, refuses an active battlefield, and claims each ended session once. An unknown result, connection loss, or a menu alone is not permission to restart. Repeating the same accepted command ID returns its receipt; a different ID cannot restart the same ended session twice.
+
+The page exports only the supported game's saved settings INI from its current runtime before release. Settings are passed as a new session overlay, and the first-launch speed default is disabled for that replay. Both Worker and main-thread paths carry the preservation flag. Imported files are not modified. The native program still initializes the skirmish and loads its scenario.
+
+This interface is separate from `ui.quickStart`, whose original reset/default behavior remains unchanged. Confirm speed, opponent settings, and native startup in a real game before enabling unattended replay. Unit tests alone do not establish this compatibility.
+
+## Local audio/video output
+
+The development canvas broadcaster can include the session's game-audio mix through `VmShell.getAudioStream()`. The audio sink owns the original stream; the broadcaster clones tracks and stops only its clones on disconnection. Session destruction releases the original capture destination. Both Worker and main-thread runtimes expose the same contract. No microphone or desktop audio is captured.
+
+The receiver must offer both video and audio transceivers and allow audio playback. Source game volume affects the transmitted mix. Do not additionally capture the same game's system/application audio in the streaming application, as that duplicates the sound. Browser autoplay restrictions may require a user gesture. Changes require refreshing both source and receiver; transport tests do not prove that the live audience heard the audio.
